target macos
om2
note hoppanel: the first cut of Hop's launcher panel as one Omega program, with no Swift. A window with a search
note field and a list. Before anything is typed the list offers up to six apps by Hop's own rule (fn swsuggest): the
note ones you usually open from the app in front or around this hour (habits.json, through fn swlifts), the ones
note you use most (recents.json), the ones that are running and the one you were just in, each with the reason. What is opened is remembered in those two files as Hop remembers it (fn urecordapp, fn urecent).
note The apps are the names in /Applications and /System/Applications. Typing narrows the
note list by Hop's own rules: the matcher (hopfuzz), the test for a web address (weba) and the order of the rows
note (swrank) are the functions of Omega/hopcore.om, which Omega/gen-ui.sh puts between the two marker notes, so
note the panel and the one box cannot drift apart. Typed text shows the eight best apps, a row that opens the text when
note it is a web address, and up to twelve files and folders from Hop's file index (file-index.bin in Hop's folder,
note which Hop builds: the panel reads it, lower-cases a copy and searches it by the rule of Hop's file search).
note The Ask row sends the typed text to a model and shows the answer as it arrives, in place of the list: the
note model is the default one of Hop's settings (config.json) when it is an HTTP one, else the
note first such; the request is Hop's (POST base/chat/completions, the model, one user message, stream true) and
note the answer's pieces are read from the server's "data:" lines. The session's delegate methods are Omega code
note (callback entries 7 and 8), called on the main queue. A model whose key is a saved account is asked with that key. The first row is selected; the down and up arrows move the selection, Enter
note opens the selected row. Like Hop it then stays: Enter and Escape hide the panel and give the app before it the
note keyboard back, and a global key (Option Space; Hop itself has Command Space), a click on its Dock icon or opening it again brings it
note back. It has a small menu (Quit, and Edit with cut, copy, paste, select all) and runs in AppKit's own event
note loop. The global key is a Carbon hot key whose handler is callback entry 5.
note The field's two delegate methods, the list's two data-source methods and the field's action are Omega code, reached
note through the callback entries (libback; omega-mv seed/ng/a64/DESIGN.md s14).
note With HOPCHECK set in the environment it checks itself instead: it stays in the background and, after half a
note second, puts "sa" into its field through the field's own editor (so AppKit, not the program, tells the
note delegate that the text changed), sends that editor arrow down, Enter and Escape as commands, prints
note "hoppanel apps N shown H first NAME selected R enter APP" and ends after six seconds. Enter reaches the field's
note action with the field as sender, as a real Enter does, but opens nothing. Status then: 1 when apps were found,
note the typed text narrowed them, the first one was read back from the list's own data source, the arrow moved the
note list's selection, Enter chose the selected app, Escape hid the panel, a hot key could be registered (a spare
note one: Control Option Shift Command F19) and its handler showed the panel again; 2 to 9 name a failed step.
note The line then ends " key K hid H back B" (K 0 when the key was registered).
note With HOPCHECK=type:TEXT it types TEXT instead and sends Enter (no arrow), still opening nothing: status 1 when
note Enter chose a row. With HOPCHECK=open:TEXT it does the same and does open the row: the line ends " opened 1"
note when the workspace took it, and the status is 1 then. With HOPCHECK=ask:TEXT it types TEXT, sends Enter on the
note Ask row (first for a question) and does ask the model: the line ends " answer TEXT" and the status is 1 when an
note answer came. With HOPCHECK=idle it types nothing and sends Enter on the first offer; HOPFRONT=ID then stands for
note the app in front. With HOPRECORD set as well, a check that opens nothing still remembers what Enter chose,
note and puts a chosen copied thing on a board of its own (the line then ends " pasted " and what is on it).
note A check reaches no other machine but through HOPSSH and HOPSCP, its stand-ins for ssh and scp (HOPSSH is
note also what a check starts in place of ssh for a forwarded port), and HOPLATE sends Enter late, when
note they have answered. With HOPCHECK=agent:TEXT a check sends TEXT to the agent of the settings and waits for it to end; with
note HOPAGENTS it first says each agent's command as it would be run.
note HOPASK2 is a second question a check asks in the same conversation once the first is answered; with
note HOPASKNEW it starts a new chat first.
note HOPSCREEN (clipboard or accounts) starts a check in that screen, and HOPCLIPDO (pin or delete) does that to
note the selected copied thing before Enter. With HOPCLIPREC a check first records what is on its own board into the history, as the program that stays
note does for the real clipboard. HOPCLIPKEY is the clipboard key for a check, in base64, or "tool:" and the name of a Keychain item of the
note security tool to read it from (the repository's checks use an item of their own, made and deleted there). HOPROW is the row Enter is sent on.
note Without HOPCHECK it runs until it is quit.
